{
  "_doc": "How each crop looks in 3D (scripts/world3d/crop_forms.gd). 'crops' gives each crop its form; one without falls back to its catalog shape. Each form has a 'kind' (which builder draws it) and that kind's numbers: heights are [low, high] in plant units (a plant is about a fifth of a tile), counts are stems or leaves, 'leaf' is the leaf colour. Produce takes the crop's own catalog colour.",
  "look": {
    "_doc": "The look every crop shares, so a field is level and shows its harvest: 'height' is how tall a ripe crop may stand, in plant units (a taller one has its stem shortened; 0: no limit), 'young' the share of that a growing one may; 'produce' is how many times bigger small produce is drawn than its form asks ('produce_small': this size or less gets all of it, 'produce_big': this size or more gets none); 'reach' is how far from its middle a plant may spread, so a plot's nine plants stay inside it and each plot is its own patch (0: no limit); 'leaf_ripe' darkens the green of a ripe crop's leaves (1: not at all), so its harvest stands out. A form with 'plot' is drawn a plot at a time (a carpet of leaves from edge to edge and 'count' big pieces of produce on it: melons, berries, peppers, heads, stakes) and not as a plant repeated in rows. A form may set its own 'produce', and 'squash': true for one that is produce from foot to tip (canes), which is drawn shorter instead of having its stem shortened.",
